<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Evripidis Bakirtzis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Euripidis Bakirtzis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Evripidis Bakirtzis was a Greek army officer known as the 'Red Colonel' for his left-wing views. A veteran of the Balkan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> wars, he joined the resistance during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation and in March 1944 became the first president of the Political Committee of National Liberation (PEEA), set up by EAM. He died in internal exile on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikaria">Ikaria</a> in 1947.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bakirtzis was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, on 16 January 1895. His father, Christos Bakirtzis, was secretary of the Greek consulate there, and his mother, the teacher Efthalias Zaka, came from a Grevena family with a military tradition reaching back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>. One of his half-brothers was a Makedonomachos fighter known as Nikos o Serraios, and another was executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> in Serres in 1916.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He entered the Hellenic Army Academy in 1911 and served as a cadet sergeant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>. During the National Schism he joined the officers of the National Defense who sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a>, distinguished himself at the Battle of Skra and received the British Distinguished Service Order. After three years at the Higher School of War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1919, he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and in 1922 took part in the Revolutionary Committee of Plastiras that overthrew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>.</p><p>His republican convictions twice brought him before the courts. In 1926 he was sentenced to death as a leader of a military coup but was not executed, and he returned to the army in 1928, later serving as military attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>. After the movement of 1 March 1935 he was again sentenced to death; the sentence was commuted to dismissal and exile on Agios Efstratios and Antikythera. He refused an offer from the Metaxas regime to serve on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> and was allowed to live in Bucharest, where he wrote studies on the military value of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the occupation he co-founded the resistance group National and Social Liberation (EKKA) with Dimitrios Psarros before joining EAM and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a>. After handing the leadership of the PEEA to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-svolos">Alexandros Svolos</a> on 18 April 1944, he commanded ELAS forces in Macedonia, and on 30 October 1944 he and Markos Vafeiadis led ELAS units into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> as the Germans withdrew, against the orders of the British general Ronald Scobie.</p><p>In September 1946 he was arrested with other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a> leaders and exiled to Agios Kirikos, then moved to Fourni. Early in 1947 the government let him testify before a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> commission on the civil war. Shortly afterwards he was found dead in his room with a bullet in his heart. His death was officially recorded as suicide, but its causes remain unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Evripidis Bakirtzis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>PEEA was led first by <b>Colonel Evripidis Bakirtzis</b> and then by the law professor Alexandros Svolos.</blockquote><a href="/state/political-committee-of-national-liberation">Political Committee of National Liberation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Fournoi islands. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.08,23.55&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Serres on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Evripidis Bakirtzis"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4585 4046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4585 4046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.08,23.55&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
